{"name":"Kernel","hex":"#ecb976","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"kernel","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The core, center, or essence of an object or system.","The central (usually edible) part of a nut, especially once the hard shell has been removed."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To enclose within a kernel","To crenellate"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/kernel"}],"entries":[{"title":"Kernel (operating system)","blocks":[{"text":"A kernel is a computer program at the core of a computer's operating system that always has complete control over everything in the system. The kernel is also responsible for preventing and mitigating conflicts between different processes. It is the portion of the operating system that is always resident in memory and facilitates interactions between hardware and software components. A full kernel controls all hardware resources via device drivers, arbitrates conflicts between processes concerning such resources, and optimizes the use of common resources, such as CPU, cache, file systems, and network sockets. On most systems, the kernel is one of the first programs loaded on startup. It handles the rest of startup as well as memory, peripherals, and input/output (I/O) requests from software, translating them into data-processing instructions for the central processing unit."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Kernel_Layout.svg/330px-Kernel_Layout.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kernel_(operating_system)","wikidata":"Q9662"},{"title":"Kernel (image processing)","blocks":[{"text":"In image processing, a kernel, convolution matrix, or mask is a small matrix used for blurring, sharpening, embossing, edge detection, and more. This is accomplished by doing a convolution between the kernel and an image. Or more simply, when each pixel in the output image is a function of the nearby pixels in the input image, the kernel is that function."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kernel_(image_processing)","wikidata":"Q1395012"},{"title":"Kernel (linear algebra)","blocks":[{"text":"In mathematics, the kernel of a linear map, also known as the null space or nullspace, is the part of the domain which is mapped to the zero vector of the co-domain; the kernel is always a linear subspace of the domain. That is, given a linear map L : V → W between two vector spaces V and W, the kernel of L is the vector space of all elements v of V such that L(v) = 0, where 0 denotes the zero vector in W, or more symbolically:"}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Projection-on-diagonal.gif/330px-Projection-on-diagonal.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kernel_(linear_algebra)","wikidata":"Q2914509"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/50/Vd-Orig.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/50/Vd-Orig.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVd-Orig.png"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/20/Vd-Rige1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/20/Vd-Rige1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVd-Rige1.png"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0b/Vd-Rige2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0b/Vd-Rige2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVd-Rige2.png"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4e/Vd-Sharp.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4e/Vd-Sharp.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVd-Sharp.png"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/04/Vd-Blur2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/04/Vd-Blur2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVd-Blur2.png"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/28/Vd-Blur1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/28/Vd-Blur1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVd-Blur1.png"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/04/Vd-Blur_Gaussian_5x5.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/04/Vd-Blur_Gaussian_5x5.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVd-Blur_Gaussian_5x5.png"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ef/Vd-Unsharp_5x5.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ef/Vd-Unsharp_5x5.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F